    "submission": "submission-candidate-client.json",
    "summary": "Turn 32 moved to a new substantive responsibility — client completion after a recovery commits a NOOP for another replica's instance — and opened it as a Candidate grounded in already-read sources.\n\nWhy this question. The recovery side is settled (claim C1: the resolution ignores a reply reporting the instance COMMITTED; claim C3: the resulting different committed vectors reach the state machines). The proposal that started the whole scenario, however, lived only in the original leader's LeaderBookkeeping, and the only re-propose path is guarded: handleCommit re-sends pending proposals only when the commit lands on the committing replica's own row (commit.Replica == r.Id) and carries exactly one NONE command, while a recovering replica preserves only its own record's clientProposals. Replies are produced at execution under the launched configuration (Dreply is hardcoded true), and a NOOP instance produces none. So the question is whether any in-repository mechanism still completes that client's operation, or whether the client waits for a reply no replica owes — a client-contract failure rather than a disagreement between replicas.\n\nHow it is framed. The principal fact is F5-pending-proposal, established by the selected behavior B1-admit-propose (primary activity A1, which also supplies the A1 relationship: the proposal's admission into decision formation), with B16-client-reply, B9-recovery-trigger and B6-commit-install named as support. Priority 2, recovery lifecycle, and the sources are the ones already acquired (handleCommit's re-propose branch and guard, handlePropose's attachment of the proposal, the recovery seed, the execution reply path, and the client-facing defs).\n\nCounterevidence and unknowns are explicit: the re-propose branch cannot fire for another replica's row; replies come from execution and a NOOP produces none; the client library's own retry behaviour is unread, so it is kept as the main unknown rather than assumed away, together with whether any other mechanism completes such a proposal and whether a recovering replica could adopt the owner's proposals.\n\nState. Audit spec version 6; the three confirmed claims are untouched; the accept-status candidate remains paused; the open scope issue on the superseded C1 artifact stays visible as bookkeeping debt, with its substantive answer already recorded in claim C3. No requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened."
